WEBVTT
TITLE Amortised Analysis: Aggregate, Accounting and Potential Methods — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
An amortised cost is not the cost of an operation. It is that operation’s share of the bill for a whole sequence.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Insert the ninth item into a table of size 8 and it copies all eight: 9 units. Insert the tenth and it costs 1.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Worst case, average case and amortised are three different questions, and only one of them uses probabilities.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Amortised is a guarantee about every sequence: whatever you do, n operations cost at most n times the amortised cost.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
A dynamic table keeps one block of slots. When the block is full, it allocates twice as many and copies everything across.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
The first nine insertions cost 1, 2, 3, 1, 5, 1, 1, 1, 9 units. Only the ninth is expensive.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
An insertion is expensive exactly when it lands on a table that is full — at item counts 1, 2, 3, 5, 9, 17, and so on.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
The expensive insertions get twice as costly and half as frequent. That trade is what makes the sequence cheap.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
The aggregate method adds the whole bill up and divides once. No cleverness, just arithmetic on the copies.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
The copy costs are 1, 2, 4, 8 and on to 512, and they add up to 1023 for 1024 insertions.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Add the 1024 writes: 1024 + 1023 = 2047 units, which is 2n - 1 for n = 1024.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Divide: 2047 over 1024 is 1.999. At most 2 units per insertion, for every sequence of that length.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
The accounting method overcharges each cheap operation and keeps the change as credit, in the same units.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
Charge 3 per insertion: 1 unit to write the item, 2 units saved. Copying one item costs 1 unit.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
When the table doubles, the banked credit pays for the copies. Over 4096 insertions the credit is never negative.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
The credit is a debt the sequence owes to the future. If it ever went below zero, the charge would be too small.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
The potential method writes the same saving as a function of the data structure: a number that goes up and down.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Take the potential to be 2 x items - capacity. It is 0 when the table is empty and never negative, because the table is always at least half full.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
Amortised cost is actual cost plus the change in potential: for the ninth insertion, 9 + (2 - 8) = 3.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
The changes telescope. Summing the amortised costs gives the total cost plus the final potential minus the first.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
A binary counter is the second classic: an increment flips a run of trailing ones to zero and then one zero to one.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Sixteen increments flip 1, 2, 1, 3, 1, 2, 1, 4, 1, 2, 1, 3, 1, 2, 1, 5 bits — 31 in all.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
The total is 2n - popcount(n): 2 x 16 - 1 = 31. The most expensive single increment still flips 5 bits.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
With the number of ones as the potential, every increment costs exactly 2 amortised: flips plus the change in ones.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
A stack supports push, pop, and multipop of k items. Each push and each pop costs one unit; multipop costs one per item removed.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
One multipop can cost 7 units while a push costs 1. Worst case, that single operation is expensive.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Every pop was paid for by an earlier push, so n operations cost at most 2n units in total.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
All 78,125 sequences of seven operations were run: the largest total is 10 units, against the bound of 14.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Amortised 2 does not mean every insertion costs 2. The 1,025th insertion copies 1,024 items and costs 1,025 units.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Nor is it an average over random inputs. No distribution appears anywhere in the argument.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Amortised always sits between the best case and the worst case. It can never be outside them.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
The claim is exact: for every sequence of n operations, the total cost is at most n times the amortised cost.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
A potential function is chosen for an operation set. Change the set and the same function can stop working.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Allow decrement: alternating between 65,535 and 65,536 flips 17 bits on every single operation.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
So increment and decrement together are O(log n) amortised, not O(1). The counter can bounce at a boundary.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
The table breaks the same way: halving at half full makes every alternating insert and delete copy 8 items.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
The most important application is union-find: alpha of n amortised per operation, at most 4 for any n below 2 to the 65536.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
The exam asks for the total over n operations, or for the amortised cost per operation, or which potential function works.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Aggregate adds the bill. Accounting saves the change. Potential telescopes a function. Same conclusion, three routes.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
The one-line test: is the expensive step paid for by work already done? If yes, the amortised cost is small.

